redfin 1256: sweettouchid applies the Touch ID set through the subsystems' setters (1247's writes all got EPERM)

1247 wrote its set with kernel_sysctlbyname(); sysctl_root() refuses any in-kernel write (from_kernel) to an OID
without CTLFLAG_KERN, and no sweet_* OID has it, so every key failed with 1 (EPERM) and stayed at its default.
Each handler of the set (kern.sweet_fpc, sweet_passcode, sweet_passcode_biostate, sweet_passcode_bio_buzz,
sweet_rpmb, sweet_mesa, sweet_mesa_match/_catacomb/_offline/_fix/_lockout/_wake/_fwd_wait_ms/_reload) is split into
<name>_kset(v) (the same checks and side effects) and a thin sysctl handler (sysctl_io_number + the setter);
sweet_fb_sleep_blank (a plain variable) is set directly. The boot thread call calls the setters in 1247's order,
kern.sweet_mesa last. New in the set: kern.sweet_mesa_wake 12 (screen-off detect + match before wake, 1255) unless
sweettouchid bits 4-7 give a value, bit 3 leaves it alone, or bit 1 asks for 5. kern.sweet_fb_sleep_blank and
kern.sweet_passcode are skipped when their own boot-args (sweetfbslp, sweetpasscode) are given.
kern.sweet_touchid_boot: "sweettouchid=0x1: OK: N ok, 0 failed, 0 skipped | fpc=0x3:ok ..." (FAILED: + errno per
key when any setter refused; PENDING / RUNNING before the thread call finished; "off" without the boot-arg).
Runtime sysctl writes behave exactly as before.

